1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
 Docker architecture.

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:05,380
 Docker architecture is client server type.

3
00:00:05,380 --> 00:00:09,080
 There are just two things which is Docker client,

4
00:00:09,080 --> 00:00:13,560
 also known as Docker CLI and Docker server.

5
00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:16,640
 This Docker server is also known by another name

6
00:00:16,640 --> 00:00:19,720
 as Docker demon or Docker.

7
00:00:19,720 --> 00:00:22,280
 So coming to the next,

8
00:00:22,280 --> 00:00:26,120
 we see this whole architecture of Docker.

9
00:00:26,120 --> 00:00:31,120
 This is the client and this is the Docker demon or Docker server.

10
00:00:31,120 --> 00:00:36,440
 So the Docker client will send these commands

11
00:00:36,440 --> 00:00:41,440
 to these Docker demon and the Docker demon will execute

12
00:00:41,440 --> 00:00:42,320
 these commands.

13
00:00:42,320 --> 00:00:46,400
 We run this commands from the command prompt of windows

14
00:00:46,400 --> 00:00:49,440
 or the terminal of Linux.

15
00:00:49,440 --> 00:00:51,440
 Once these commands are run,

16
00:00:51,440 --> 00:00:56,600
 the client will take these commands to the Docker demon

17
00:00:56,600 --> 00:01:00,560
 and the Docker demon will execute these commands.

18
00:01:00,560 --> 00:01:04,280
 These commands can be creating images, containers,

19
00:01:04,280 --> 00:01:07,320
 and doing other things like Docker,

20
00:01:07,320 --> 00:01:10,800
 pull commands, Docker, build, Docker run,

21
00:01:10,800 --> 00:01:12,880
 are different types of commands.

22
00:01:12,880 --> 00:01:16,080
 We will understand more about commands,

23
00:01:16,080 --> 00:01:19,800
 the containers images later on.

24
00:01:19,800 --> 00:01:22,520
 Now we will understand Docker image.

25
00:01:22,520 --> 00:01:26,000
 A Docker image is a template for running containers.

26
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:31,000
 For example, Microsoft.net is provided as a Docker image.

27
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:36,200
 Similarly, Microsoft SQL server is also provided

28
00:01:36,200 --> 00:01:38,400
 as a Docker image.

29
00:01:38,400 --> 00:01:42,480
 Our Docker images could be engine X, web server,

30
00:01:42,480 --> 00:01:47,120
 post-grads SQL, red days, PHP and Python,

31
00:01:47,120 --> 00:01:51,280
 etcetera, etcetera. We can create a Docker image

32
00:01:51,280 --> 00:01:55,520
 for our app on.net, version 10,

33
00:01:55,520 --> 00:02:00,520
 based on newborn to OS along with engine X, web server.

34
00:02:00,520 --> 00:02:05,520
 We can create another image of our app on version.net 9.0,

35
00:02:05,520 --> 00:02:13,960
 which is based on Windows OS along with engine X, web server.

36
00:02:13,960 --> 00:02:16,280
 So these can be two images.

37
00:02:16,280 --> 00:02:21,040
 Similarly, dear could be many more images of our app.

38
00:02:21,040 --> 00:02:25,280
 So let us understand how images are built.

39
00:02:25,280 --> 00:02:29,800
 So images are built on layers.

40
00:02:29,800 --> 00:02:33,960
 And there are many layers in an image.

41
00:02:33,960 --> 00:02:37,480
 For example, there is a base OS image,

42
00:02:37,480 --> 00:02:42,480
 which could be a Linux or a Windows or Mac OS.

43
00:02:43,120 --> 00:02:48,120
 And then there could be another layer of runtime,

44
00:02:48,120 --> 00:02:53,440
 which could be.net runtime, version.net 10,

45
00:02:53,440 --> 00:02:57,960
 or runtime.net 9, etcetera.

46
00:02:57,960 --> 00:03:02,680
 There could be dependencies, which our app needs.

47
00:03:02,680 --> 00:03:07,680
 These could be JSON.net package or the entity framework,

48
00:03:07,680 --> 00:03:12,680
 code package, etcetera, etcetera.

49
00:03:12,680 --> 00:03:16,840
 And application code is our.net app,

50
00:03:16,840 --> 00:03:20,880
 which we have built and configuration files

51
00:03:20,880 --> 00:03:25,480
 could be the another image layer

52
00:03:25,480 --> 00:03:28,800
 for the configuration files of our app.

53
00:03:28,800 --> 00:03:32,240
 Images are very lightweight and very fast

54
00:03:32,240 --> 00:03:35,800
 because they are built on layers.

55
00:03:35,800 --> 00:03:38,400
 When we do some changes on them,

56
00:03:38,400 --> 00:03:42,520
 then only the layers that have changed are be rebuilt,

57
00:03:42,520 --> 00:03:44,400
 rest remains the same.

58
00:03:44,400 --> 00:03:47,840
 This makes the image lightweight and very fast.

59
00:03:47,840 --> 00:03:49,360
 To build an image,

60
00:03:49,360 --> 00:03:51,960
 we will need to have a Docker file,

61
00:03:51,960 --> 00:03:53,880
 which is a text file,

62
00:03:53,880 --> 00:03:58,520
 providing all the instructions needed for the image to build.

63
00:03:58,520 --> 00:04:03,520
 Let us see the Docker file of a.net version 10 app.

64
00:04:03,520 --> 00:04:06,920
 So this is the Docker file.

65
00:04:06,920 --> 00:04:11,200
 Each instruction here will create a layer.

66
00:04:11,200 --> 00:04:16,200
 For example, this instruction from mcr.microsoft.com,

67
00:04:16,200 --> 00:04:19,200
 etcetera will create a layer.

68
00:04:19,200 --> 00:04:21,680
 Another instruction is this one.

69
00:04:21,680 --> 00:04:24,200
 This will also create a layer.

70
00:04:24,200 --> 00:04:27,200
 And this third will also create a layer.

71
00:04:27,200 --> 00:04:31,200
 Similarly, the fourth one will also create a layer.

72
00:04:31,200 --> 00:04:34,760
 Docker file will also refer to external files

73
00:04:34,760 --> 00:04:37,680
 and libraries needed for the app to run.

74
00:04:37,680 --> 00:04:42,680
 For example, it is referring to SDK version 10 of.net

75
00:04:42,680 --> 00:04:47,240
 and ASP.net version 10.

76
00:04:47,240 --> 00:04:52,240
 It can also refer to other dependencies like newtons of JSON

77
00:04:52,240 --> 00:04:58,640
 or entity framework or needed for the app to run,

78
00:04:58,640 --> 00:05:01,600
 all will be listed on this Docker file.

79
00:05:01,600 --> 00:05:03,920
 So when the image was built,

80
00:05:03,920 --> 00:05:06,560
 it will download these dependencies

81
00:05:06,560 --> 00:05:10,600
 so that the app can run Docker container.

82
00:05:10,600 --> 00:05:15,240
 A Docker container is an object that runs the images.

83
00:05:15,240 --> 00:05:17,320
 For example, these are the images

84
00:05:17,320 --> 00:05:21,440
 and Docker container will be needed to run them.

85
00:05:21,440 --> 00:05:25,200
 A Docker container could be running.net image.

86
00:05:25,200 --> 00:05:29,520
 Another Docker container could be running MS SQL Server image.

87
00:05:29,520 --> 00:05:33,880
 Another Docker container could be running engine X image,

88
00:05:33,880 --> 00:05:37,160
 Redis image, etcetera, etcetera.

89
00:05:37,160 --> 00:05:40,960
 We have Docker commands for creating containers,

90
00:05:40,960 --> 00:05:44,920
 running containers, deleting containers and so on.

91
00:05:44,920 --> 00:05:47,200
 Docker registries.

92
00:05:47,200 --> 00:05:50,360
 Registries store Docker images online

93
00:05:50,360 --> 00:05:53,240
 so that people can download these Docker images

94
00:05:53,240 --> 00:05:57,840
 of software's ARMD's registries.net image

95
00:05:57,840 --> 00:06:01,800
 and MS SQL image, engine X image, etcetera,

96
00:06:01,800 --> 00:06:05,360
 all kept on Docker registries.

97
00:06:05,360 --> 00:06:08,760
 Example of these registries are Docker hub

98
00:06:08,760 --> 00:06:11,560
 and Azure container registry.

99
00:06:11,560 --> 00:06:14,800
 On Google type Docker hub

100
00:06:14,800 --> 00:06:21,560
 and click on the search result.

101
00:06:21,560 --> 00:06:26,080
 Here we can search microsoft.

102
00:06:28,880 --> 00:06:33,880
 These are the Microsoft images of different products.

103
00:06:33,880 --> 00:06:40,080
 You will find MS SQL Server image here.

104
00:06:40,080 --> 00:06:44,960
 Then here are other images,

105
00:06:44,960 --> 00:06:49,960
 including the dot net image needed for our dot net app to build.

106
00:06:49,960 --> 00:06:56,640
 So you can also create an account here

107
00:06:56,640 --> 00:07:01,640
 and you can save your app images on Docker Hub.

108
00:07:01,640 --> 00:07:05,800
 Other than that,

109
00:07:05,800 --> 00:07:10,800
 you can also search for is your container registry.

110
00:07:10,800 --> 00:07:22,920
 You can create account on Azure to use these container registry.

111
00:07:25,760 --> 00:07:28,840
 We can understand the whole process from this image.

112
00:07:28,840 --> 00:07:33,840
 Programmer a creates a dot net image for his app

113
00:07:33,840 --> 00:07:37,840
 and pushes this image to Docker hub registry.

114
00:07:37,840 --> 00:07:41,360
 Programmer B now wants to download this image

115
00:07:41,360 --> 00:07:43,640
 from this Docker hub registry

116
00:07:43,640 --> 00:07:46,800
 and she can easily download the image

117
00:07:46,800 --> 00:07:51,560
 and the image reaches her own local laptop

118
00:07:51,560 --> 00:07:55,000
 and she can create a container of this image

119
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:57,840
 and run the dot net app

120
00:07:57,840 --> 00:08:01,000
 which is produced by Programmer A.

